messages:
lina: quick q — we have a customer hit where our JSON endpoint sometimes returns a key as null and other times just omits it. client SDK treats missing as undefined, null as explicit. should we standardize?

matt: can you paste the endpoint? is this for streaming or non-streaming?

lina: /v1/generate (structured output mode). non-streaming first. sample: `{"choices": [{"content": null}]}` vs `{"choices": [{}]}`

devin: hmm. historically we said optional fields = omitted, nullable = explicit null only for semantic difference. but infra has been returning null for empty arrays sometimes.

matt: sdk folks prefer omission for absent values (so they can use default props). explicit null suggests user intention. both are valid but inconsistent behavior causes regressions.

lina: short-term: what's the least-breaking change? can we map null->omit on the hosted API for v2?

devin: we could add a compatibility flag on the route to drop nulls when sending to clients, but that may mask bugs. better to make consistent at generation layer: if a field is semantic-optional, omit; if it's explicitly nullable type, allow null. need a doc update.

matt: proposal: 1) canonicalization step pre-response that removes keys with null if schema marks them optional (not nullable). 2) add Content-Type enforcement: `application/json; charset=utf-8` for structured responses. 3) for streaming ndjson, continue sending `application/x-ndjson` and preserve nulls as-is and document differences.

lina: +1 on 1 and 2. Can you write the canonicalization rule? small snippet maybe.

matt: pseudo:```js
function canonicalize(obj, schema) {
  for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(obj)) {
    const prop = schema.properties?.[k];
    if (v === null && prop && prop.type !== 'null' && !(Array.isArray(prop.type) && prop.type.includes('null'))) {
      delete obj[k];
    } else if (typeof v === 'object' && v !== null) {
      canonicalize(v, prop || {});
    }
  }
  return obj;
}
```

devin: looks fine. edge: empty string vs null — we shouldn't drop empty strings. only null.

lina: how do we signal this to SDKs? need a changelog + canary header so clients can opt-in to strict mode.

matt: add response header `X-Structured-Canonical: v1` when canonicalization applied. SDKs can opt into expecting omission. default for hosted should use canonicalization for non-streaming.

devin: agree. also add an integration test in the contract suite: request sample prompt -> assert absent keys rather than null when schema says optional.

lina: will you file the repo PRs? I can pick up SDK note.

matt: filing now — PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/platform/pull/5423 (draft). includes unit tests and header.

lina: awesome. one more: content-type quirks — legacy clients ignore charset. should we always emit `charset=utf-8`?

devin: yes. set `Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8` and include `Vary: Accept` only if we do content-negotiation. we are not changing streaming types.

matt: added that change to the PR.

sdk-bot: note: SDK v1.4.0 will treat missing fields as undefined; v1.5.0 will check for `X-Structured-Canonical` header and strip runtime nulls accordingly.

lina: timeline? customer wants a canary today if possible.

matt: canary in us-west-2 for 2% traffic by EOD if infra approves. tagging eng-oncall.

devin: i'll add the contract test to the nightly and a quick smoketest.

lina: thanks. assigning follow-ups: matt -> platform PR + canary deploy; devin -> contract tests + infra ticket; lina -> SDK docs + customer note. :thumbsup:

matt: acked. :shipit:

devin: acked.

lina: closing loop here — will post rollback plan in the PR comments.
